    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a significant distinction. So, you're essentially saying that the \"social utility\" and the predictability of their environment act as decisive factors in their medical priority. If we were to apply this logic to future triage scenarios, would this protocol inadvertently create a cycle where individuals from lower-income or more transient backgrounds are consistently deprioritized because their \"environmental stress\" or \"lack of wellness checkups\" makes them a less \"efficient\" investment of medical resources compared to those in high-income brackets?",
